OWASP Top 10 Web Application Vulnerabilities

A01: Broken Access Control: Restrictions on what authenticated users are allowed to do are not properly enforced.

A02: Cryptographic Failures: Failures related to encryption (or lack thereof) leading to sensitive data exposure.

A03: Injection: Flaws like SQL, NoSQL, or OS injection where untrusted data is sent to an interpreter.

A04: Insecure Design: Risks resulting from missing or ineffective security control design in architecture.

A05: Security Misconfiguration: Missing appropriate security hardening across any part of the application stack.

A06: Vulnerable and Outdated Components: Using software components with known vulnerabilities in production.

A07: Identification and Authentication Failures: Weaknesses related to confirming a user's identity or session management.

A08: Software and Data Integrity Failures: Code and infrastructure failing to protect against integrity violations (e.g., untrusted CI/CD or updates).

A09: Security Logging and Monitoring Failures: Insufficient logging and missing integration with incident response.

A10: Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF): Flaws allowing an application to send crafted requests to an unexpected destination.



