## General Scanning Workflow (Putting It Together)

| Phase | Tools |
|---|---|
| **Recon / fingerprinting** | Wappalyzer, WhatWeb, `nmap`, Shodan |
| **Content/endpoint discovery** | gobuster, ffuf, dirsearch, Burp Spider |
| **Automated vulnerability scanning** | OWASP ZAP, Burp Suite Pro, Nikto, Nessus |
| **Dependency/component scanning** | OWASP Dependency-Check, Snyk, Trivy, Grype |
| **Manual/business-logic testing** | Burp Suite (Repeater/Intruder), manual auth flow walkthroughs |
| **Cloud/infra config audit** | Scout Suite, Prowler, CIS benchmarks |
| **Out-of-band/blind vuln detection** | Burp Collaborator, interact.sh |

**A practical testing methodology** most pentesters follow (aligned with **OWASP Testing Guide** / **PTES**):
1. Recon & fingerprint the stack.
2. Automated baseline scan (ZAP/Nikto) to catch low-hanging fruit (A05, A06, A02 headers).
3. Authenticated crawl with two privilege levels â†’ test A01 (access control) systematically.
4. Fuzz all input points for A03 (injection) with sqlmap/Intruder.
5. Manual business-logic and auth-flow testing for A04/A07.
6. Dependency/SCA scan for A06.
7. Review CI/CD and serialization handling for A08.
8. Trigger test events and confirm detection for A09.
9. Test every URL-accepting feature for A10 with Collaborator-style callbacks.

**Legal note:** Only run these scans/tests against systems you own or have explicit written authorization (e.g., a signed pentest engagement or bug bounty scope) to test â€” unauthorized scanning of third-party systems is illegal in most jurisdictions.

